  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["J1", "J6"],
      "pins": ["J1.14", "J6.4"],
      "nets": ["M-BUS", "PORTB2", "PIN14"],
      "observed": "On the single sheet, a wire runs from J1 pin 14 (PIN14) diagonally up to the global label 'M-BUS' and continues into J6 pin 4, which carries the PORTB2 label. J2 pin 14 has no such wire, so J1 and J2 are not identical pass-through connectors, and the unrelated bus pin 14 is merged onto the PortB unit signal line.",
      "expected": "Spec: J1/J2 are 'identical nets on both so the board can be stacked between the M5Stack Core and a lower module (pass-through)', and J6 is 'M5Unit PortB breakout (GND, +5V, two GPIOs), passed through from J1/J2' — i.e. J6 pin 4 should sit on the PORTB2 net (J1/J2 pin 3) only, and J1 pin 14 should pass through to J2 pin 14 unaltered.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "diagonal wire from J1 PIN14 (pin 14) to global label 'M-BUS' terminating at J6 pin 4 (PORTB2); J2 pin 14 has no corresponding wire (sheet m5-pantilt, columns 3-4)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "J6 | Conn_01x04 | M5Unit PortB breakout (GND, +5V, two GPIOs), passed through from J1/J2."},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "Top and bottom M5Stack bus connectors; identical nets on both so the board can be stacked between the M5Stack Core and a lower module (pass-through)."}
      ],
      "impact": "The PortB unit connector's signal pin is shorted to M5Stack bus pin 14 (an unrelated GPIO), and the J1→J2 pass-through for pin 14 is broken; plugging an M5Unit PortB device would load/conflict with GPIO14 and the stacked module above would not see the expected net.",
      "confidence": 0.45
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Most of the drawing matches the spec (jumper network, MAX3485 auto-direction circuit, diode-OR power paths, servo rail buffering, resistor/capacitor values), but an extra wire ties J1 pin 14 through the 'M-BUS' global label into J6 pin 4 (PORTB2), merging an unrelated bus GPIO into the PortB unit line and breaking the J1/J2 pass-through identity — a connectivity anomaly."
}

